{"kind":{"type":"paragraph"},"runs":[{"text":"A real ⌘. (command flag only) fires ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"\".\"","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" + ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":".command","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" and does not fire ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"\".\"","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" + ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"[.command, .shift]","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":". So both definitions catch ⇧⌘., AppKit prefers the unshifted-plus-shift item when both exist, and the shortcut does not collide with ⌘. on the same key. ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"performKeyEquivalent(with:)","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" matches on more than the two character fields. A hand-built event that copies the real event's ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"characters","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" (","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"\".\"","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"), ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"charactersIgnoringModifiers","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" (","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"\">\"","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"), key code, and flags still misses the ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"\".\"","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" + shift item and fires ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"\">\"","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" + ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":".command","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":". So a hand-built event cannot stand in for a real keystroke even when you copy its fields.","style":[],"link":null,"image":false}],"quoteDepth":0,"listDepth":0,"marker":null},
{"kind":{"type":"paragraph"},"runs":[{"text":"Key code 47 is the period key's position on an ANSI keyboard. On another layout the same key code produces other characters, and the CGEvent probe reports them, which is the point: it answers for the layout the machine has.","style":[],"link":null,"image":false}],"quoteDepth":0,"listDepth":0,"marker":null},
